<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      `allowBackup` stays true and the rules files decide what that means.

      Turning it off outright would have been the smaller change, and it would
      also have closed the door on the thing worth having: a person who *wants*
      their portfolio backed up, and says so. What was wrong was never that
      backup existed — it was that it happened to everybody, silently, with the
      unencrypted database in it. So: backup is on, and the rules exclude
      everything except a directory that is empty until somebody opts in. See
      `res/xml/backup_rules.xml`.

      Both files are named because they cover different Android versions and
      neither is a fallback for the other: `dataExtractionRules` is read on API
      31 and above, `fullBackupContent` below it, and minSdk here is 24.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      Two permissions removed for Google Play, and neither costs a feature.

      REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS used to be declared here so the app
      could show Android's own one-tap "let this run in the background"
      dialog. Play prohibits that permission unless doze breaks the core
      function of the app, and the acceptable list is short and specific —
      calling apps, safety apps, task automation, peripheral companions. A
      price-alert tracker is not on it, and arguing that it is would be
      arguing in bad faith: the app checks alerts every time it is opened, so
      doze delays them rather than breaking them.

      What is left is the same destination one tap further away.
      `BatteryOptimizationPlugin` opens the battery-optimisation *list*, which
      needs no permission at all, and reading the current state
      (`isIgnoringBatteryOptimizations`) never needed one either. So the
      screen still knows whether Android is holding the check back, and still
      offers a way out; it just cannot grant it in one tap.

      SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM arrives from @capacitor/local-notifications and
      @capacitor/background-runner, both of which support scheduling a
      notification for a *time*. This app never does: every call to
      `LocalNotifications.schedule` omits the `schedule` field, which posts
      immediately and sets no alarm. Play treats the permission as restricted
      and expects a declaration naming an alarm-clock or calendar use case
      this app does not have. Removing it removes nothing that runs.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      Location, removed.

      @capacitor/background-runner declares ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION,
      ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION and ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION in its own manifest,
      because the runner can be used for geofencing. This app uses it for one
      thing — waking every half hour to compare prices — and never asks where
      the phone is.

      The merger would fold them in silently, and they would show on the
      install screen and in the app's permission list. For an app whose whole
      argument is that nothing about the portfolio leaves the device, shipping
      a background-location permission it never calls is not a cosmetic
      problem: it is the app contradicting itself in the one place a person
      actually checks.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
